# The Pane - a glazier, a broken window, and which side the glass is on.
# Written for the library. One person, one fact, and the question of whether a fact is yours to say.

title: The Pane
start: start

== start ==
bg east_corner
music room_tone
You have been glazing for thirty years and you can read a broken window the way other
people read a face. Which pane went first. Whether it was hit or leaned on. Whether the
glass is on the inside or the outside of the frame, and that one is the whole of it,
because glass goes the way the force went.
You are called out for insurance work, mostly, and mostly it is what the form says.
-> the_call

== the_call ==
The house is a bungalow at the end of a road of bungalows and the call is from a man
who says his mother's back door was put through last night and can you come today.
You can come today. You are there by half past nine with a van full of glass and a
wooden metre stick you have had since 1994.
-> the_house

== the_house ==
The back door is half glazed and the bottom pane is gone and the gap has been boarded
with a piece of hardboard and two screws, which somebody did last night and did well.
There is glass on the kitchen floor. There is also glass on the step outside, and the
step outside is under a porch, and there is no wind in this county that puts glass
under a porch.
The man makes the tea and tells you what happened while you sweep up.
-> the_story

== the_story ==
man: Half eleven, they think. She was in bed, she heard it, she came down and it was like
this, and nothing's been taken.
man: The police came out and did a report and said they'd be in touch, and the insurance
want a glazier's invoice and a crime number, so that's what I'm getting.
His mother is sitting at the kitchen table with her hands around the cup and she has not
said anything at all, and she is watching you sweep, which people do.
-> the_reading

== the_reading ==
You pick up four pieces off the floor and you look at them the way you look at four
pieces, and then you look at the frame, and then at the step, and then at the frame again.
Everything about the door says one thing and everything about the floor says the same
thing, and it is not the thing in the story: whatever went through this pane went through
it from the kitchen side, and the pane folded outwards, and the pieces on the step are
the pieces that would do that.
-> the_decision

== the_decision ==
The man is at the sink with his back to you telling you about the crime number. His
mother is at the table with her hands around the cup.
You have a piece of glass in your hand and a metre stick under your arm and the hardboard
is leaning against the wall where you took it down, and the invoice in your pocket has a
line on it that says CAUSE OF BREAK, which is a line the insurance asks for and which you
have filled in four hundred times and never once thought about.
* Say nothing. Fit the glass. Write "forced entry" on the line. -> say_nothing
* Tell the man, plainly, once, with the pieces in your hand. -> tell_the_man
* Ask the mother what happened, with him out of the room. -> ask_her
* Write what you can see on the line and let the form say it. -> the_line

== say_nothing ==
You fit the glass. It takes forty minutes and it is a good fit and you put the hardboard
in the van because the man says he does not want it and somebody might as well have it.
You write FORCED ENTRY on the line and he pays you in cash and thanks you for coming out,
and his mother says thank you as well, from the table, and it is the only thing she says.
-> ending_fitted

== tell_the_man ==
you: I have to tell you what I can see, because it's on the form.
man: Go on.
you: The glass went outwards. It was broken from this side, from the kitchen, and the
pieces on the step are the pieces that would be there if it was.
He looks at the door for a long time. He does not look at his mother and she does not look
at him, and the two of them do the same thing at the same moment, which is nothing.
-> the_after

== the_after ==
man: Right.
you: That's what I can see. What you put on the form is yours.
man: Right.
He pays you and you go, and you have fitted the glass and you have said the true thing
once and you have not said it twice, which is the whole of what a person in a van can do
about a kitchen.
-> ending_said_it

== ask_her ==
You ask the man to give you a hand with a sheet of glass from the van, which is a lie, and
he goes, and the back door stays open, and you carry on sweeping.
woman: It was me.
She says it to the floor. You do not stop sweeping and you do not say anything, because
there is a way of doing this and it is to keep sweeping.
woman: He'd been on at me about the will since Easter. I didn't want him going through the
drawer again. I put my hand through it and then I sat down and then I thought about it for
an hour before I rang him.
-> the_told_me

== the_told_me ==
You put the glass in and it is a good fit and the man comes back with the sheet you did
not need and says where do you want it, and you say the van, and he puts it in the van.
The mother asks you for the invoice and you give it her and she reads the line that says
CAUSE OF BREAK and then she looks at you, and you have to decide what is on that line
while she is looking at you.
-> ending_hers

== the_line ==
You fit the glass and you take the invoice out and you write, on the line that says CAUSE
OF BREAK, four words that are true and that are not an accusation: GLASS WENT FROM INSIDE.
The man reads it at the door and goes very still, and then he folds it in half and puts it
in his inside pocket and pays you, and says thank you for coming out, and closes the door.
-> ending_the_line

== ending_fitted ==
The insurance pays it. That is what the form is for and that is what the line is for, and
you have never once been asked a question about it in thirty years and you will not be
asked one about this either.
You think about the woman at the table for a while, on the drive home, and then you stop
thinking about her, because there are two more calls on the sheet and the second one is in
the next town.
-> END

== ending_said_it ==
You said the true thing once and you did not say it twice, and that is the whole of the
job you could do, and it is more than most of the trade would have done.
Six weeks later you are called back to the same road for a different house and you look at
the bungalow as you go past. The pane is in. Somebody has painted the frame, which nobody
asked them to do, and which is the kind of thing people do when they have decided something.
-> END

== ending_hers ==
You do not know what she wrote. You never see the form again and you are not the person it
goes to and there is no version of the next ten years in which you find out.
What you know is that she read the line and looked at you and then took the pen off the
ledge herself and wrote on the invoice in her own hand, and that she did it without asking
you what to put, and that she was steady about it, and that her son was in the van with a
sheet of glass he did not need.
-> END

== ending_the_line ==
The four words go to the insurance company and you never hear anything about them, because
an insurance company is a machine that reads a line and prices a risk and does not ring a
glazier.
You fit glass in that road three more times that winter and you never see the man again,
and once you see the mother in the shop and she looks at you for exactly as long as it
takes to decide, and then she nods, and you nod, and that is the entire conversation, and
it is the one you think about.
-> END
